\section{Preserve Exact Integers Across a Number-Only Host API}

Suppose an application passes integer identifiers through a JavaScript interface that accepts either a number or a tagged decimal string, but not \texttt{BigInt}. This is an application wire contract, not the behavior of the Gambit interface described above. Let \(M=2^{53}-1\). A host number may carry an integer in the inclusive range \([-M,M]\); otherwise an exact Scheme integer travels in the record \verb|{decimal: "..."}|. Require canonical signed decimal notation with no leading zeros, plus sign, exponent, or negative zero. Given a value in one of these two representations, the reader returns an exact Scheme integer or a defined error. Assume an implementation able to represent all integers in the accepted string range.

\begin{verbatim}
encode(n):
  require n is an exact integer
  if -M <= n <= M: return number(n)
  # Convert directly from exact arithmetic.
  return record(decimal = format_exact(n))
decode(v):
  if v is a number:
    # Reject ambiguity already present at the boundary.
    if not safe_integer(v): return BadNumber
    return exact_integer(v)
  if v is not an accepted decimal record: return BadTag
  if not canonical_decimal(v.decimal): return BadDigits
  return parse_exact(v.decimal)
\end{verbatim}

For \(9007199254740991\), encoding yields a number and decoding yields that same exact integer. Encoding \(9007199254740993\) instead puts the string \texttt{"9007199254740993"} in the \texttt{decimal} field, and decoding preserves the last digit. If a foreign caller supplies the latter identifier as a JavaScript number, it has already rounded to \(9007199254740992\) and decoding reports \texttt{BadNumber}; the wrapper cannot recover the lost bit. A decimal field containing \texttt{"003"} gives \texttt{BadDigits}. Numeric negative zero has no distinct result in the chosen Scheme integer model.

The invariant is exact round-trip equality for values the encoder admits, without any narrowing conversion on the decimal path. The string representation requires work and allocation dependent on digit count; a service can bound that count on both sides if it declares an explicit size error. This test exercises the marshalling semantics independently of the handle and callback lifetime checks.
